NOTICE
======

This project (code: MIT, see LICENSE) uses data from several public sources.
Their attribution and reuse terms differ, and this file records how each is
handled.

ENTSO-E Transparency Platform
  Data: electricity load and day-ahead load forecast for the DE-LU zone
  (milestones 6 and 7).
  Terms: published for free re-use under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
  International licence (CC-BY 4.0); attribution to ENTSO-E is required.
  https://transparency.entsoe.eu
  Handling: raw series are gitignored; the committed result JSONs
  (wc_load_results.json, wc_permutation_results.json) contain aggregated
  analysis derived from this data, not the raw series.

aWATTar day-ahead price API
  Data: EPEX SPOT day-ahead wholesale prices, passed through by aWATTar
  (milestones 1-5, 8, 10, and the day-ahead side of R9).
  https://www.awattar.de/services/api
  Handling: raw price CSVs (prices.csv, year_prices.csv, wc_prices.csv) are
  gitignored; committed outputs are derived aggregates.

Open-Meteo
  Data: weather (temperature, cloud, wind, shortwave radiation), historical
  archive and forecast, used for the comparable-day matching.
  Terms: Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC-BY 4.0).
  https://open-meteo.com
  Handling: raw weather CSVs are gitignored.

netztransparenz.de (reBAP)
  Data: the German uniform imbalance price (reBAP), the real-time settlement
  price, published by the four German transmission system operators (R9).
  Terms: NO explicit reuse licence is provided; statutory norms apply.
  https://www.netztransparenz.de
  Handling: because no reuse licence is granted, the raw reBAP series is treated
  as NOT redistributable. It is fetched locally only (intraday_prices.csv is
  gitignored) and is never committed. Two committed files hold reBAP-derived
  analysis, and both contain aggregates only, never the source dataset:
    - intraday_results.json (R9): median spreads by hour of day and season,
      volatility ratios, correlation, cheapest-hour overlap counts, and the
      regret concentration curve. All pooled over hundreds of days.
    - wc_intraday_results.json (R3): per-match-day effect estimates, permutation
      aggregates, the whole-day drift diagnostic, and the calendar split.
  One reconstruction risk is handled explicitly rather than left to chance.
  wc_results.json commits the day-ahead price per match day and hour window. Had
  wc_intraday_results.json also published the absolute reBAP-minus-day-ahead
  spread for those same days and windows, the two committed files could be added
  together to recover the reBAP level (checked: exact on some days, about
  1.4 ct/kWh mean error across the set). So wc_intraday.py publishes only the
  effect delta per day, which is a difference of differences measured against
  control days that are never published, and therefore cannot reconstruct a
  level. The absolute per-day spreads stay local.

Author-compiled inputs
  wc_matches.csv (match schedule) and events_holidays.csv (German public
  holidays) were compiled by the project author from public information.

This notice is a good-faith record of data provenance, not legal advice. Where a
source provides no explicit licence, this project errs toward keeping the raw
data local and committing only its own derived analysis.
